## ⚙️ Edge‑Optimized Persistence & Sync

Edge‑driven collaboration hinges on two tightly coupled concerns: **local durability** that survives intermittent connectivity, and **global convergence** that guarantees every participant eventually observes the same CRDT state. This section walks through the concrete Java building blocks that make the pattern work, explains the runtime flow, and contrasts alternative persistence strategies in a concise table.

### 1. Domain Model – CRDT Core Types  

The domain model isolates the *operation* semantics from transport and storage concerns. By using a small hierarchy of interfaces, an abstract base, and concrete CRDT implementations we keep the model extensible while preserving type‑safety.

    // Marker for any CRDT‑enabled aggregate
    public interface CrdtAggregate {
        String getId();
        CrdtState snapshot();
        void apply(CrdtOperation op);
    }

    // Common fields for all CRDTs
    public abstract class BaseCrdt implements CrdtAggregate {
        protected final String id;
        protected final List<CrdtOperation> pendingOps = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();

        protected BaseCrdt(String id) { this.id = id; }

        @Override public String getId() { return id; }

        @Override public CrdtState snapshot() {
            // Concrete subclasses decide how to materialise a snapshot
            return buildSnapshot();
        }

        protected abstract CrdtState buildSnapshot();
    }

    // Concrete text‑editing CRDT (a simplified RGA)
    public class TextCrdt extends BaseCrdt {
        private final StringBuilder content = new StringBuilder();

        public TextCrdt(String id) { super(id); }

        @Override public void apply(CrdtOperation op) {
            if (op.type() == OperationType.INSERT) {
                content.insert(op.position(), op.payload());
            } else if (op.type() == OperationType.DELETE) {
                content.delete(op.position(), op.position() + op.length());
            }
            pendingOps.add(op);
        }

        @Override protected CrdtState buildSnapshot() {
            return new TextState(id, content.toString(), new ArrayList<>(pendingOps));
        }
    }

    // Enum describing the primitive operation kinds
    public enum OperationType { INSERT, DELETE, FORMAT }

    // Immutable operation record (Java 17+)
    public record CrdtOperation(
        OperationType type,
        int position,
        String payload,
        int length,
        UUID opId,
        Instant timestamp) {}

    // Snapshot container – can be serialized to JSON or protobuf
    public sealed interface CrdtState permits TextState {}
    public final record TextState(String id, String text, List<CrdtOperation> history) implements CrdtState {}

### 2. Spring REST Endpoint – Edge API  

Edge nodes expose a thin HTTP façade that lets browsers push local ops and pull the latest snapshot. The controller delegates to a service that orchestrates Axon event sourcing, Kafka publishing, and Resilience4j guards.

    @RestController
    @RequestMapping("/edge")
    public class EdgeSyncController {

        private final EdgeSyncService syncService;

        public EdgeSyncController(EdgeSyncService syncService) {
            this.syncService = syncService;
        }

        // Push a locally generated operation
        @PostMapping("/{docId}/ops")
        public ResponseEntity<Void> pushOp(@PathVariable String docId,
                                           @RequestBody CrdtOperation op) {
            syncService.handleLocalOp(docId, op);
            return ResponseEntity.accepted().build();
        }

        // Pull the latest snapshot (used after reconnection)
        @GetMapping("/{docId}/snapshot")
        public ResponseEntity<CrdtState> getSnapshot(@PathVariable String docId) {
            CrdtState snapshot = syncService.fetchSnapshot(docId);
            return ResponseEntity.ok(snapshot);
        }
    }

### 3. Service Layer – Axon + Kafka + Resilience4j  

The `EdgeSyncService` wires three responsibilities:

1. **Axon Event Store** – persists every operation as an event, enabling replay for new edge nodes.
2. **Spring Kafka Producer** – fan‑out to a topic (`crdt-ops`) that all edge instances subscribe to.
3. **Resilience4j** – applies a retry policy (max 3 attempts) and a circuit‑breaker (failure‑rate > 50 % opens the breaker for 30 s).

    @Service
    public class EdgeSyncService {

        private final EventGateway eventGateway;          // Axon
        private final KafkaTemplate<String, CrdtOperation> kafkaTemplate;
        private final Retry retry;
        private final CircuitBreaker circuitBreaker;
        private final Map<String, TextCrdt> localCache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

        public EdgeSyncService(EventGateway eventGateway,
                               KafkaTemplate<String, CrdtOperation> kafkaTemplate,
                               RetryRegistry retryRegistry,
                               CircuitBreakerRegistry cbRegistry) {
            this.eventGateway = eventGateway;
            this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
            this.retry = retryRegistry.retry("kafkaPublish");
            this.circuitBreaker = cbRegistry.circuitBreaker("kafkaPublish");
        }

        public void handleLocalOp(String docId, CrdtOperation op) {
            TextCrdt crdt = localCache.computeIfAbsent(docId, TextCrdt::new);
            crdt.apply(op);
            // Persist via Axon
            eventGateway.publish(op);
            // Asynchronously publish to Kafka with resilience
            Supplier<Void> publish = () -> {
                kafkaTemplate.send("crdt-ops", docId, op);
                return null;
            };
            Try.ofSupplier(Retry.decorateSupplier(retry, publish))
               .recoverWithFallback(ex -> {
                   circuitBreaker.onError(0, ex);
                   return Try.failure(ex);
               });
        }

        public CrdtState fetchSnapshot(String docId) {
            return localCache.computeIfAbsent(docId, TextCrdt::new).snapshot();
        }

        // Kafka consumer – updates local cache on remote ops
        @KafkaListener(topics = "crdt-ops", groupId = "edge-group")
        public void onRemoteOp(@Header("key") String docId,
                               @Payload CrdtOperation op) {
            TextCrdt crdt = localCache.computeIfAbsent(docId, TextCrdt::new);
            crdt.apply(op);
        }
    }

### 4. Sync Flow – Step‑by‑Step  

1. **User edits** → UI creates a `CrdtOperation` and POSTs to `/edge/{docId}/ops`.  
2. **Controller** forwards to `EdgeSyncService.handleLocalOp`.  
3. **Axon** records the operation in the event store, guaranteeing durable replay.  
4. **Resilience4j** decorates the Kafka publish; on transient network glitches the retry loop re‑attempts, while the circuit‑breaker shields the thread pool from cascading failures.  
5. **Kafka** broadcasts the op to every edge instance (including the originator, which discards duplicates via op‑ID tracking).  
6. **Listener** receives remote ops, applies them to the local CRDT, and updates the in‑memory cache.  
7. **When connectivity returns** after a partition, the edge node queries `/edge/{docId}/snapshot`. The service returns the latest snapshot, built from the persisted event stream, allowing the client to fast‑forward its local state.

### 5. Persistence Strategy Comparison  

| Strategy | Durability | Conflict‑Resolution Overhead | Network Footprint | Typical Latency (ms) |
|----------|------------|------------------------------|-------------------|----------------------|
| **Axon + Kafka (CRDT)** | Event store + replicated topic → strong durability | CRDT merges are O(1) per op, no version vectors | Small JSON per op (≈ 200 B) | 5 – 15 (local) |
| **Local‑Only SQLite** | Persists only on the edge device → vulnerable to loss on device failure | No merge needed (single writer) | Zero remote traffic | < 1 (offline) |
| **Redis Cache + Pub/Sub** | Volatile memory; requires snapshotting to disk | Requires external version vectors → higher CPU | Pub/Sub messages similar to Kafka | 2 – 8 (in‑cluster) |
| **Cassandra Wide‑Row** | Tunable consistency (QUORUM) → higher durability | Requires tombstone handling for deletes | Larger payloads (binary blobs) | 15 – 30 (cross‑DC) |

The CRDT‑backed Axon/Kafka combo offers the sweet spot for ultra‑low‑latency edge collaboration: **instant local visibility**, **automatic convergence**, and **resilient delivery** without sacrificing durability.

### 6. Key Takeaways  

* **Local cache + event store** gives immediate read/write without round‑trips.  
* **Resilience4j** protects the publish path, ensuring that temporary edge‑to‑cloud outages do not stall the UI.  
* **Kafka as a durable log** guarantees at‑least‑once delivery; CRDT idempotence makes duplicate handling trivial.  
* **Axon** abstracts event sourcing, letting new edge nodes replay the full history to rebuild any missing state.  

By marrying these open‑source primitives, the Edge‑Optimized Persistence & Sync layer delivers the ultra‑low‑latency guarantees required for shared‑state applications that run at the network edge.

