## A[User Edit] -->|Local CRDT Update| B[Edge Node State]

When a user types or deletes text in the browser, the client emits a lightweight *EditEvent* that travels over WebSocket to the nearest edge node. The edge node is the first point of truth for that user’s session, so the edit must be reflected immediately in its local CRDT replica before any replication to the cloud.

### 1. Capture and translate the event

The edge exposes a Spring REST controller that accepts the raw edit payload.  
```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/edits")
class EditController {

    private final CommandGateway commandGateway;

    @PostMapping("/{docId}")
    public Mono<ResponseEntity<Void>> receiveEdit(@PathVariable String docId,
                                                 @RequestBody EditDto dto) {
        // Convert DTO to a domain command
        EditCommand cmd = new EditCommand(docId, dto.getUserId(),
                                          dto.getOperation(), dto.getPosition(),
                                          dto.getText());
        return commandGateway.send(cmd)
                .then(Mono.just(ResponseEntity.ok().build()));
    }
}
```
The controller is stateless; all business logic lives in the command handler.

### 2. Dispatch via Axon with resilience

`CommandGateway` is wrapped by Resilience4j’s `@CircuitBreaker` and `@Retry` annotations so that transient network hiccups or back‑pressure from downstream services do not block the user.  
```java
@CommandHandler
@CircuitBreaker(name = "edgeNode", fallbackMethod = "fallback")
@Retry(name = "edgeNode")
public void handle(EditCommand cmd) {
    // Retrieve or create the in‑memory CRDT
    ORSetCRDT<String> crdt = crdtStore.getOrCreate(cmd.getDocId());
    crdt.apply(cmd.getOperation(), cmd.getText(), cmd.getPosition());
    // Persist state snapshot for crash recovery
    crdtStore.save(crdt);
    // Publish an event for remote replication
    eventBus.publish(new EditAppliedEvent(cmd.getDocId(), crdt.getVersionVector()));
}
```
The fallback simply logs the failure and returns a 503; the retry policy retries up to three times with exponential back‑off.

### 3. Local CRDT mutation

The `ORSetCRDT` implements the generic `CRDT` interface.  
```java
interface CRDT<T> {
    void apply(Operation op, T value, int position);
    VersionVector getVersionVector();
}

abstract class AbstractCRDT<T> implements CRDT<T> {
    protected final Set<T> elements = new HashSet<>();
    protected final VersionVector vv = new VersionVector();
}

enum Operation { ADD, REMOVE }

class ORSetCRDT<T> extends AbstractCRDT<T> {
    @Override
    public void apply(Operation op, T value, int position) {
        switch (op) {
            case ADD -> {
                elements.add(value);
                vv.incrementLocal();
            }
            case REMOVE -> elements.remove(value);
        }
    }
}
```
The `VersionVector` is a simple map of node IDs to counters, incremented on every local mutation. This vector is embedded in the `EditAppliedEvent` so that downstream replicas can merge deterministically.

### 4. Merge logic (DSA)

When a replica receives an `EditAppliedEvent`, it merges the incoming vector with its own.  
```java
void merge(VersionVector incoming) {
    for (Map.Entry<String, Long> e : incoming.getMap().entrySet()) {
        vv.get(e.getKey()).max(e.getValue());
    }
}
```
Three key algorithms are exposed: `applyAdd`, `applyRemove`, and `merge`, each O(1) on average for the OR‑Set.

### 5. Persistence and eventual consistency

The edge node writes a snapshot of the CRDT to local disk (e.g., a JSON file). This guarantees that a crash does not lose the most recent user edits. The `EditAppliedEvent` is sent to Apache Kafka, where a downstream “replication” service consumes it and forwards the mutation to other edge nodes.

