## B -->|Eventual Broadcast| C[Peer Edge Nodes]

When a mutation reaches the `EditCommandHandler`, the local OR‑Set is updated and a snapshot is persisted. The next phase is to *eventually* inform every peer edge node so that their replicas converge. This broadcast stage is the linchpin that guarantees eventual consistency while preserving the ultra‑low‑latency guarantees of the local update path.

### 1. Resilience‑Aware Kafka Producer

The `BroadcastService` wraps a Spring Kafka `KafkaTemplate` and is guarded by a Resilience4j circuit breaker. The circuit breaker is configured with a failure threshold of 5 % and a 30 s reset timeout, ensuring that transient network glitches do not cascade into a global outage. The producer serializes the `EditEvent` into a compact JSON payload that contains the operation type, the CRDT payload, and a monotonically increasing `sequenceId`. The `sequenceId` is generated by a thread‑safe `AtomicLong` that is persisted in the local store so that a node can recover its last published event after a restart.

```java
public interface BroadcastService {
    void publish(EditEvent event);
}
```

```java
@Component
public class KafkaBroadcastService implements BroadcastService {
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;
    private final CircuitBreaker breaker;
    private final AtomicLong seq = new AtomicLong();

    public KafkaBroadcastService(KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate,
                                 CircuitBreakerRegistry registry) {
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
        this.breaker = registry.circuitBreaker("broadcast");
    }

    @Override
    public void publish(EditEvent event) {
        event.setSequenceId(seq.incrementAndGet());
        String payload = JsonMapper.toJson(event);
        breaker.executeRunnable(() -> kafkaTemplate.send("peer-edits", payload));
    }
}
```

The `EditEvent` interface is a marker for all CRDT‑based mutation events. Concrete implementations include `AddElementEvent`, `RemoveElementEvent`, and `MergeEvent`. Each carries a `Set<UUID>` of element identifiers and a `Map<UUID, Long>` of timestamps to support fast conflict resolution.

### 2. Peer‑to‑Peer Consumption

Peers consume the `peer-edits` topic via a Spring Kafka listener. The listener is idempotent: it stores the `sequenceId` of the last processed event in a local `ConcurrentSkipListSet`. If a duplicate arrives (common in at‑least‑once delivery), the listener simply discards it. The event is then merged into the local OR‑Set using the `CRDTMergeStrategy`:

```java
public interface CRDTMergeStrategy<T> {
    T merge(T local, T remote);
}
```

```java
@Component
public class OrSetMergeStrategy implements CRDTMergeStrategy<ORSet> {
    @Override
    public ORSet merge(ORSet local, ORSet remote) {
        Set<UUID> added = new HashSet<>(local.getAdded());
        added.addAll(remote.getAdded());
        Set<UUID> removed = new HashSet<>(local.getRemoved());
        removed.addAll(remote.getRemoved());
        return new ORSet(added, removed);
    }
}
```

The merged state is written back to the local store and the UI is notified via a WebSocket push, keeping the user’s view in sync without any blocking network round‑trip.

### 3. Three Java Problem Solutions

| Problem | Solution | Key Idea |
|---------|----------|----------|
| Idempotent consumption | Store `sequenceId` in a `ConcurrentSkipListSet` and skip duplicates | Guarantees at‑least‑once semantics |
| Conflict resolution | Use a timestamp‑based CRDT merge strategy | Linearizes concurrent edits |
| Backpressure handling | Apply a bounded queue to the Kafka listener and drop or replay events based on `maxQueueSize` | Prevents memory blow‑up during network partitions |

### 4. Spring REST Endpoint for Peer Discovery

Peers can discover each other through a lightweight REST API. The `PeerDiscoveryController` exposes a `/peers` endpoint that returns the list of known peers. When a new node joins, it registers itself by POSTing its address to this endpoint. The controller is annotated with `@RestController` and uses a `PeerRegistry` bean to maintain the list.

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/peers")
public class PeerDiscoveryController {
    private final PeerRegistry registry;

    public PeerDiscoveryController(PeerRegistry registry) {
        this.registry = registry;
    }

    @GetMapping
    public List<PeerInfo> list() { return registry.getAll(); }

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<Void> register(@RequestBody PeerInfo info) {
        registry.register(info);
        return ResponseEntity.accepted().build();
    }
}
```

